---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';
---

<Base title="About XIS Controls" description="Engineers and innovators. Designing turnstile control systems since 1981.">
  <div class="wrap about">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>About</nav>
    <h1>We not only sell the parts. We know how the stuff works.</h1>
    <div class="about__grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          In 1981 we began designing custom circuit and logic solutions for the access control industry. Under the name Hard
          Logic, we were the force behind the electronics and logic controls in turnstiles built by Tomsed Corporation, and
          we designed many of the original, replacement and upgrade controllers still running today.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tomsed acquired several other companies and their product lines over the years, including the Philips (formerly
          Robot and Burle) turnstile business in 2001. Support for all of those legacy products now lives here at XIS Controls.
        </p>
        <p>
          We're not part of any other company in this business, and never have been. If you've seen our photos on someone
          else's site, they were copied. If they can't sell you the part, call us. We probably make it.
        </p>

        <h2>How it started</h2>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            In 1977, while working for a major security integrator in New York City, I met Thomas Sedivy. Tom was working for
            Perey in those days and his feeling was that he could do the turnstile product in a much more reliable, secure,
            aesthetic and cost effective way. Tom and I would cross paths many times and we became friends. In 1981 Tomsed
            (yes, a concatenation of his name) opened its doors and he asked me to be his electrical designer. Tomsed
            established themselves as the number one in the turnstile business in the USA in a short period of time.
          </p>
          <footer>Al Gershman</footer>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>From the former President and CEO of Tomsed</h2>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            I have known Al Gershman and his prior company, Hard Logic, since my first days at TOMSED. He designed many of our
            control circuits and was always available for special projects and help with any customer's unique needs. To see
            now that Al has established XIS Controls with the specific goal of supporting the entire range of TOMSED and
            Philips products gives me great comfort knowing that our loyal customers will once again receive the service they
            deserve.
          </p>
          <footer>Robert T. Sedivy</footer>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Al Gershman</h2>
        <p>
          As Chief Engineer at ISSCO Corp., Al designed security for the Metropolitan Museum, the Frick Collection, many Wall
          Street firms, and the system that followed "The Treasures of King Tut" on its US tour. As Principal Research Engineer
          for Keyspan Energy he built a fiber optic campus network, access control for underground regulator vaults and SCADA
          hardware. As Director of Research and Development at Tomsed he led one of the industry's first optical turnstiles and
          all of its turnstile control systems. He is a former President and CEO of ENTELEC, the Energy Telecommunications and
          Electrical Association.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="side">
        <figure class="lillie">
          <img src="/media/philips-burle-parts/lillie-and-mouse-inpixio.jpg" alt="Lillie, a tabby cat, with a toy mouse" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Meet Lillie, head of Quality Catrol.</strong> Born in 2006 in Lillington, NC, she patrolled the factory floor
            at Tomsed and kept it clear of mice, until new owners decided she was a nuisance. She was scooped up and brought to
            Fuquay-Varina, where nothing leaves our facility without her meow of approval.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="contact">
          <h2>Get in touch</h2>
          <p class="contact__phone"><a href={site.phoneHref}>{site.phone}</a></p>
          <p><a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></p>
          <p>{site.address[0]}<br />{site.address[1]}</p>
          <p>Email any time, including after hours and weekends. Please include your name and phone number.</p>
          <p class="hint">D-U-N-S {site.duns}</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-bottom: 72px;
  }
  .about h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.6rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
  .about__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(24px, 5vw, 64px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: 36px;
  }
  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 1em;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 20px;
    border-left: 6px solid var(--yellow);
  }
  blockquote footer {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .lillie {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    background: var(--bench);
    padding: 14px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .lillie figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
  }
  .contact {
    border: 2px solid var(--black);
    border-left: 10px solid var(--yellow);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 16px 18px;
  }
  .contact h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .contact__phone a {
    font-family: var(--f-cond);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 2rem;
    color: var(--black);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .about__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
